{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_b46d5126c3f7686d","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## TASK 1 — Where the \"expected size 2560\" raise came from\n\n**Locations found.** The string appears (18x) in\n- `/home/qiba/ROCm.AI/hyperloom/session/Ornith-1.5-397B-FP8/20260915T161838Z-ba2694ea/reports/enablement/9d27140cf07749878ef698ad2789b20d/server.log:605` (first hit), mirrored in `.../runs/integrate_patch/684650cc04404308aed3e22cffc3ed0e/variant_00_integrate-patch-9d27140c/benchmark_vllm_20260915_170328/server.log`, and quoted inside `.../runs/specialist/{fc07edbde4c9489682f2cafa5a7d253a,c89d4bf43b6f46d2b23c41a68257ef0e}/process.log`. It does **not** appear in the old session `20260915T150443Z-6b7d597b` (grep -r: 0 hits). Error text:\n  `AssertionError: expected size 2560==4096, stride 4096==1 at dim=0; expected size 4096==2560, stride 1==4096 at dim=1`\n\n**Deepest traceback frames (prefix stripped):**\n```\nmultiproc_executor.py:1039 worker_busy_loop -> output = func(...)\ngpu_worker.py:519 determine_available_memory -> self.model_runner.profile_run()\ngpu_model_runner.py:6618 profile_run -> :6266 _dummy_run -> outputs = self.model(...)\nqwen3_5.py:573 forward -> hidden_states = self.language_model.model(...)\ncompilation/decorators.py:573 -> cuda_graph.py:254 -> piecewise_backend.py:380/198\ntorch/_inductor/output_code.py:725 __call__ -> return self.current_callable(inputs)\n/root/.cache/vllm/torch_compile_cache/torch_aot_compile/63eba2e7.../inductor_cache/sz/cszdeqcj...py:2234 in call\n    assert_size_stride(arg10_1, (4096, 2560), (1, 4096))\nAssertionError: expected size 2560==4096, ...\n```\n\n**Answer to your question: neither.** The raise came from a **torch inductor AOT-compiled guard** (`assert_size_stride` in the generated `cszdeqcj....py:2234`), reached through the compiled forward of `qwen3_5.py:573` during `profile_run` — not from `emulation.py`, and not from a Python frame in linear.py / the weight loader / compressed_tensors. I grepped the whole log: `emulation.py` frames appear **zero times** in any traceback; the file only appears as INFO load messages (`server.log:192+`: `\"Emulating FP8 w8a8 dense linears with bfloat16 GEMM on this GPU ... dequantizing weights at load time\"`). In the inductor cache the guarded arg feeds `extern_kernels._scaled_mm(buf22, arg10_1, ...)` — a compiled path expecting a col-major `[4096,2560]` FP8 weight but receiving row-major `[2560,4096]`, i.e. some layer (source-node list shows an act-quant + `_scaled_mm` fusion, consistent with the MoE/shared-expert side that emulation.py explicitly does not cover) bypassed the emulation kernel and kept an FP8 weight that the compiled graph then transposes-checks.\n\n## TASK 2 — emulation.py A (new) vs B (old), and other diffs\n\n`diff -u` is 156 lines; function-level:\n- **Docstring**: reworded; B adds measured cost rationale (`~7-21 TFLOPS` FP8 Triton vs `~103-133 TFLOPS` BF16; `~10 GB ... ~1.3 GB per GPU at TP=8`). Both state only *dense* linears take the path — **MoE experts keep their own kernels** — in both versions.\n- **`can_implement`**: A has an extra gate `if c.activation_quant_key.scale.group_shape.is_per_group(): return False, \"cannot implement block/group-wise FP8 activations.\"` — B **dropped** it.\n- **`_dequant` (shape/layout handling)**: logic **identical** in both — `w_nk = self._as_nk(weight)` then `if w_nk.stride(-1) != 1: w_nk = w_nk.contiguous()`, cast to fp32, per-N/per-K scale broadcast, same RuntimeError fallback. Only cosmetic restructuring of the scalar-scale branch + a docstring `[N, K]` note differ.\n- **`input_quant_key`**: present in A (`return None` to block fusion hoisting FP8 act-quant upstream) — **removed in B**.\n- **`process_weights_after_loading`**: identical core (`replace_parameter(... weight ...)`, `layer.weight.input_dim = 1; layer.weight.output_dim = 0`); A **additionally** overwrites `weight_scale` with scalar ones (\"The scale has been